{
  "teamId": 140,
  "year": 2025,
  "narrative": "The 2025 Texas Rangers entered the season carrying the weight of championship expectations but quickly found themselves fighting for respectability rather than hardware. Early April provided flashes of the team's offensive ceiling — Jonah Heim delivering clutch hits and Adolis García announcing himself with a go-ahead two-run home run on April 19 — but the bullpen, particularly Luke Jackson, was already fraying at the seams, surrendering several high-leverage blows that kept the Rangers from pulling away in winnable games.\n\nThe summer months told a story of two teams coexisting in one clubhouse. Adolis García and Corey Seager provided genuine star-level moments, with García's June 30 three-run blast and Seager's 10th home run of the year serving as the kind of plays that fuel playoff dreams. Yet for every highlight, reliever Robert Garcia seemed to be on the mound for the counterpunch — surrendering home runs to Isaac Paredes, Gunnar Henderson, J.P. Crawford, and Randy Arozarena in a string of costly outings that defined the team's inability to close out opponents and string together winning stretches.\n\nAugust brought the season's most turbulent stretch. Back-to-back days saw Ketel Marte victimize the Texas bullpen with consecutive home runs, the second a devastating three-run shot off Phil Maton that swung the largest single win probability in the entire season against the Rangers. Joc Pederson and Josh Jung provided back-to-back homers on August 4th to remind everyone of the team's power potential, and Rowdy Tellez emerged as a reliable middle-of-the-order presence, but the bullpen's fragility meant too many promising nights slipped away.\n\nThe final weeks of the season were a mirror of everything that had come before — individual heroics from Wyatt Langford and Alejandro Osuna offering hope, while Jose Corniell's three-run home run surrendered in late September served as a grim coda to a frustrating year. Texas finished 81-81, their pitching staff posting a quietly impressive 3.47 ERA that deserved a better fate, undermined by a lineup that too often went quiet and a bullpen that couldn't protect leads when it mattered most. The 2025 Rangers were a .500 team in nearly every sense — talented enough to believe, inconsistent enough to disappoint.",
